          %h1#serre_functor Serre functor
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Serre functor
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} exact functor
            such that for any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Hom(A,B) \simeq \Hom(B,S(A))^{\ast}
            from their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-object"} hom-object
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+dual"} linear dual
            of their reverse hom-object, “twisted” by the Serre functor.
          %p A Serre functor does not always exist, but when it does then it is unique up to graded natural isomorphism.
          %p
            The notion of Serre functors was introduced by
            %a{:href => "#BondalKapranov89"} Bondal &amp; Kapranov 1989
            to study
            %span.newWikiWord
              admissible subcategories
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/admissible+subcategories"} ?
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories"} triangulated categories
          %p
            Serre functors have become a powerful tool for working with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaves"} coherent sheaves
            on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variety"} variety
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p In the original paper, the following definition was given.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              be a
              = succeed "-linear" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hom"} Hom
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              algebraically closed. A
              %strong Serre functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{A}
              is an additive equivalence that commutes with the translation functor, with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphisms"} natural
              (in both variables)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{A,B} \colon \Hom_\mathcal{A}(A,B) \stackrel{\sim}{\to} \Hom_{\mathcal{A}}(B,S(A))^*
              for any objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo ,
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi^{-1}_{B, S(A)})^* \circ \phi_{A,B} : \Hom(A,B) \to \Hom(B,S(A))^* \to \Hom(S(A), S(B))
            %p
              coincides with the isomorphism induced by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            In fact, the last commutativity condition can be deduced from just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} naturality
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{A,B}
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              be a
              = succeed "-linear" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hom"} Hom
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              an arbitrary field. A
              %strong Serre functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{A}
              is an additive equivalence with bi-functorial isomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hom
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{A,B} : \Hom_\mathcal{A}(A,B) \stackrel{\sim}{\to} \Hom_{\mathcal{A}}(B,S(A))^*
              for any objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p Of course, formally the definition could be used in categories enriched over a symmetric monoidal category with a sufficiently nice involution.
          %h2#examples Examples
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              In the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite-dimensional vector spaces
              over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              In the derived category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaves"} coherent sheaves
              on a smooth projective
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variety"} variety
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\cdot \otimes \omega_X)[n]
              is a Serre functor, in view of
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Serre-Grothendieck duality
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Serre-Grothendieck+duality"} ?
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_X
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+sheaf"} canonical sheaf
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is the dimension of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h2#properties Properties
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Any autoequivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{A}
              commutes with a Serre functor: there is a natural graded isomorphism of functors
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi S
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo ∼
                      %mi S
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \circ S \stackrel{\sim}{\to} S \circ F
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Any Serre functor in a
              %span.newWikiWord
                graded category
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/graded+category"} ?
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  graded
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/graded"} ?
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Any Serre functor in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
              is
              %span.newWikiWord
                exact
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/exact"} ?
              (i.e.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distinguished+triangles"} distinguished triangles
              are mapped to distinguished triangles).
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              Any two Serre functors are connected by a canonical graded functorial isomorphism that commutes with the isomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{A,B}
              in the definition of the Serre functor.
          %h2#references References
          %p The original paper and English translation:
          %ul
            %li#BondalKapranov89
              %p
                А. И. Бондал, М. М. Капранов,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Представимые функторы, функторы Серра и пе­рестройки
                %a{:href => "http://www.mathnet.ru/links/99950da8b286808c64286a2820235651/im1153.pdf"} pdf
            %li#BondalKapranov90
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexei+I.+Bondal"}> Alexei I. Bondal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+M.+Kapranov"} Mikhail M. Kapranov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Representable functors, Serre functors, and mutations
                %strong 35
                3 (1990) 519-541 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1070/IM1990v035n03ABEH000716"} doi:10.1070/IM1990v035n03ABEH000716
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.mathnet.ru/links/6ad8b932b7c8801d1b821034af7d08b2/im1153_eng.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The following paper gives the corrected definition and also demonstrates the utility of the Serre functor as a tool for working with the derived category of coherent sheaves on a variety (c.f.
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bondal-Orlov+reconstruction+theorem"} Bondal-Orlov reconstruction theorem
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aleksei+Bondal"}> Aleksei Bondal
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Orlov"} Dmitri Orlov
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.mi.ras.ru/~orlov/papers/Compositio2001.pdf"} Reconstruction of a variety from the derived category and groups of autoequivalences
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Oleksandr Manzyk,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A-infinity-bimodules and Serre A-infinity-functors
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://kluedo.ub.uni-kl.de/files/1910/dissertation.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "https://kluedo.ub.uni-kl.de/files/1910/dissertation.djvu"} djvu
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Serre
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
                  functors
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.irb.hr/korisnici/zskoda/manzyukslides.pdf"} pdf
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Volodymyr+Lyubashenko"} Volodymyr Lyubashenko
            %li
              Volodymyr Mazorchuk, Vanessa Miemietz,
              %em Serre functors for Lie algebras and superalgebras
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sefi+Ladkani"}> Sefi Ladkani
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categorification of a linear algebra identity and factorization of Serre functors
              %strong 285
              (2017) 879–896
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00209-016-1731-9"} doi
